  <h1>Types of Traumatic Brain Injuries</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Greer Holiday (GH), Editor-in-Chief &mdash; Neurotrauma Practice. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>Traumatic brain injury is not a single disease. It is a category that covers a wide range of injury patterns, mechanisms, and outcomes. Understanding the specific type of injury matters for prognosis, treatment, and (in litigation) damages valuation. This page summarizes the classification systems used in clinical and litigation contexts and describes the major injury types.</p>

  <h2>Severity classification: mild, moderate, severe</h2>

  <p>The primary severity classification is based on Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) at admission, often combined with loss of consciousness (LOC) duration and post-traumatic amnesia (PTA) duration.</p>

  <h3>Mild TBI (GCS 13&ndash;15)</h3>

  <p>Also called concussion. Defined by GCS 13&ndash;15 at admission, LOC of less than 30 minutes (or no LOC), and PTA of less than 24 hours. Typical findings on standard CT are negative. Most patients recover within weeks to months, but a meaningful minority (often estimated at 15&ndash;30%) develop persistent post-concussive syndrome with symptoms lasting months to years. Mild TBI cases are litigation-significant precisely because the injury is invisible on standard imaging while producing real cognitive, mood, and somatic symptoms.</p>

  <p>Common symptoms: headache, dizziness, light and noise sensitivity, sleep disturbance, irritability, memory and concentration problems, fatigue, and (in some patients) mood symptoms including depression and anxiety. Vestibular and oculomotor symptoms (balance issues, convergence insufficiency) are also common and respond to specialized therapy.</p>

  <h3>Moderate TBI (GCS 9&ndash;12)</h3>

  <p>GCS 9&ndash;12 at admission, LOC of 30 minutes to 24 hours, PTA of 1&ndash;7 days. Standard CT often shows abnormalities (contusion, small hemorrhage, edema). Patients require inpatient hospitalization, often followed by inpatient rehabilitation. Outcomes vary widely: some patients recover near-normal function over 12&ndash;24 months; others retain permanent cognitive, behavioral, or motor deficits. Vocational outcome varies with pre-injury occupation.</p>

  <h3>Severe TBI (GCS 3&ndash;8)</h3>

  <p>GCS 3&ndash;8 at admission, LOC of more than 24 hours, PTA of more than 7 days. Standard CT typically shows significant abnormalities. Patients require intensive care, often with neurosurgical intervention. Outcomes range from substantial recovery (rare but documented for some severe TBI patients) to persistent cognitive and motor disability to vegetative or minimally conscious state. Most severe TBI patients have meaningful permanent deficits and require ongoing rehabilitation and supportive care.</p>

  <h3>Catastrophic outcomes</h3>

  <p>A subset of severe TBI patients end in vegetative state (unaware and unresponsive but with sleep-wake cycles), minimally conscious state (intermittent awareness with reproducible but inconsistent purposeful behavior), or locked-in syndrome (preserved consciousness with profound motor paralysis, typically due to brainstem injury). These outcomes require lifetime 24-hour skilled care and produce the highest verdict and settlement totals.</p>

  <h2>Focal vs diffuse injury</h2>

  <p>Beyond the severity score, TBI is classified by the spatial pattern of injury.</p>

  <h3>Focal injuries</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Contusion.</strong> Bruising of the cortical surface, typically at the site of impact (coup) and at the opposite side of the brain (contrecoup). Contusions are visible on CT and MRI. They produce deficits in the function localized to the affected region (e.g., frontal-lobe contusion may produce executive function deficits).</li>
    <li><strong>Subdural hematoma.</strong> Bleeding between the dura mater and the brain. Most common in the elderly (with cerebral atrophy stretching the bridging veins) and in patients on anticoagulants. Acute subdural hematomas can produce severe injury through mass effect and herniation. Chronic subdural hematomas can develop weeks after seemingly minor trauma.</li>
    <li><strong>Epidural hematoma.</strong> Bleeding between the skull and the dura, typically from a torn middle meningeal artery in the context of a temporal skull fracture. Classic "lucid interval" followed by deterioration. Surgical evacuation can produce excellent outcomes if performed quickly; delayed diagnosis produces severe outcomes.</li>
    <li><strong>Subarachnoid hemorrhage.</strong> Blood in the subarachnoid space following trauma. Often present alongside other injury patterns.</li>
    <li><strong>Intracerebral hemorrhage.</strong> Bleeding within the brain parenchyma itself.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diffuse injuries</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Diffuse axonal injury (DAI).</strong> Stretching and shearing of axons throughout the brain, typically from rotational acceleration (high-speed motor vehicle accidents, falls from height). Standard CT often appears normal or shows only small petechial hemorrhages; MRI (particularly susceptibility-weighted imaging and DTI) reveals the true injury extent. DAI produces diffuse cognitive impairment that is often disproportionate to the imaging findings on standard CT. DAI is a major reason why mild and moderate TBI cases can produce significant deficits despite "normal" imaging.</li>
    <li><strong>Hypoxic / anoxic brain injury.</strong> Diffuse injury from oxygen deprivation. Causes include cardiac arrest, near-drowning, strangulation, severe respiratory failure, and (in obstetric contexts) intrapartum hypoxia. Anoxic injury preferentially affects watershed areas and the hippocampus, often producing significant memory impairment and (in severe cases) vegetative outcomes.</li>
    <li><strong>Diffuse cerebral swelling.</strong> Brain-wide edema following trauma, more common in pediatric TBI. Can produce severe injury through elevated intracranial pressure even without significant focal hemorrhage.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Primary vs secondary injury</h2>

  <p>The clinical and forensic distinction between primary and secondary injury is important.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Primary injury</strong> is the damage done by the mechanical event itself: the contusion at the point of impact, the diffuse axonal stretch, the epidural hematoma from the skull fracture. Primary injury is fixed at the moment of trauma.</li>
    <li><strong>Secondary injury</strong> is damage that develops in the hours and days after the initial event: ischemia from hypotension or hypoxia, mass effect from expanding hematoma, cerebral edema, herniation, seizure-related injury, and the inflammatory cascade. Secondary injury is preventable with timely and appropriate medical management; preventable secondary injury is a common medical-malpractice theory in TBI cases.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Neuroimaging in TBI</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>CT (non-contrast head CT).</strong> The acute-care workhorse. Excellent for detecting acute hemorrhage, skull fracture, and gross mass effect. Insensitive to DAI, small contusions, and brainstem injury.</li>
    <li><strong>MRI.</strong> More sensitive than CT for non-hemorrhagic injury, particularly DAI and brainstem injury. Susceptibility-weighted imaging (SWI) detects small hemorrhagic foci characteristic of DAI. Diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) can detect white-matter injury invisible on standard MRI sequences but remains controversial as forensic evidence in some jurisdictions.</li>
    <li><strong>Functional imaging (fMRI, PET, SPECT).</strong> Used in research and some clinical contexts; admissibility in litigation varies by jurisdiction.</li>
    <li><strong>EEG.</strong> Important for seizure detection and prognosis in severe TBI.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A critical clinical and litigation point: a normal CT does not rule out TBI. Mild TBI and DAI commonly produce significant symptoms with a normal CT. Patients with persistent symptoms and a negative CT often benefit from MRI with appropriate sequences, and from neuropsychological evaluation.</p>

  <h2>Secondary syndromes after TBI</h2>

  <h3>Post-concussive syndrome (PCS)</h3>

  <p>Persistent symptoms beyond the expected recovery window after mild TBI. Symptoms include headache, dizziness, sleep disturbance, cognitive difficulty, mood disturbance, and fatigue. PCS is a real diagnosis with a substantial peer-reviewed literature, but historically faced skepticism from defense experts. Current consensus is that a meaningful minority of mild TBI patients develop PCS and that the syndrome can be disabling. Documentation of symptoms with validated symptom inventories and neuropsychological testing supports PCS claims in litigation.</p>

  <h3>Post-traumatic seizures and epilepsy</h3>

  <p>Seizures can occur immediately after TBI (impact seizures), in the first week (early post-traumatic seizures), or beyond (post-traumatic epilepsy). Risk of post-traumatic epilepsy is elevated in penetrating injuries, depressed skull fractures, severe TBI with hematoma, and severe TBI generally. Antiseizure medication management is often a long-term care item.</p>

  <h3>Post-traumatic depression and anxiety</h3>

  <p>Mood and anxiety disorders are common after TBI. The mechanism is part neurological (frontal-lobe injury affecting mood regulation), part psychological (response to disability and life disruption), and part medication-related. Treatment is essential and is a recurring item in life-care plans.</p>

  <h3>Chronic traumatic encephalopathy (CTE)</h3>

  <p>Progressive neurodegenerative process associated with repeated head trauma. Currently diagnosable definitively only at autopsy. Significant in the context of contact sports (football, boxing, MMA) and military service with repeated blast exposure. CTE is increasingly relevant in litigation involving repeated concussion exposure, including the major NFL and NHL concussion settlements.</p>

  <h3>Second impact syndrome</h3>

  <p>Rare but catastrophic condition in which a second concussion sustained before recovery from a first concussion produces diffuse cerebral swelling and severe outcome. Most prominent in pediatric and adolescent athletes. Return-to-play protocols in organized sports are designed to prevent second impact syndrome.</p>

  <h2>Causes of TBI</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Motor vehicle accidents</strong> &mdash; the leading cause of severe TBI in adults under 65. Includes occupant injuries, motorcycle riders (where helmet status is litigation-relevant), pedestrians, and cyclists. Rotational acceleration from MVA is a leading mechanism of DAI.</li>
    <li><strong>Falls</strong> &mdash; the #1 cause of TBI overall and the leading cause in the elderly. Falls produce both focal (subdural hematoma in elderly on anticoagulation) and diffuse injuries.</li>
    <li><strong>Workplace</strong> &mdash; construction, falls from height, struck-by-object incidents. Workers' compensation typically covers the employer, with third-party claims against equipment manufacturers or premises owners adding recovery.</li>
    <li><strong>Sports and recreation</strong> &mdash; football, hockey, soccer, equestrian, cycling. Single severe events plus repeated subconcussive exposure.</li>
    <li><strong>Assault</strong> &mdash; including domestic violence and shaken baby syndrome.</li>
    <li><strong>Medical</strong> &mdash; obstetric hypoxic-ischemic injury, anesthesia-related hypoxic events, post-cardiac-arrest anoxic injury.</li>
    <li><strong>Military</strong> &mdash; blast injury produces a distinctive TBI pattern relevant to veterans claims.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Pediatric TBI</h2>

  <p>Children are not small adults for TBI purposes. Pediatric TBI has distinct mechanisms (shaken baby syndrome, falls from changing tables), distinct imaging considerations (developing brain, open sutures), distinct prognosis patterns (some better recovery, some worse particularly for very young brains), and distinct litigation considerations (longer life of damages, larger lost-earnings claims, conservatorship and special needs trust planning).</p>

